%U A401691 16120329358251123855322583040000,8567052315466410266168054821109760000,13658674249908300014018650170720639221760000,65329056494432401534650811244352037219779870720000
%N A401691 Number of stabilizer states on n qutrits.
%C A401691 In quantum information theory, a stabilizer state on n qutrits (three-level quantum systems) is a common eigenstate of an abelian subgroup of order 3^n of the n-qutrit generalized Pauli (Weyl-Heisenberg) group.
%C A401691 Equivalently, a(n) is the number of affine Lagrangian (maximal isotropic) subspaces in the 2n-dimensional symplectic vector space GF(3)^(2n).
%C A401691 The number of linear Lagrangian subspaces in GF(3)^(2n) is Product_{i=1..n} (3^i + 1) = A290000(n+1) (Gross, 2006). This count can also be derived via the orbit-stabilizer theorem from the order of the finite symplectic group Sp(2n, 3) (Dickson, 1901).
%C A401691 Each linear Lagrangian subspace has 3^n cosets (affine translations). For odd primes, the discrete Wigner function of a stabilizer state is proportional to the indicator function of an affine Lagrangian subspace. Thus, these cosets correspond bijectively to the stabilizer states, yielding 3^n distinct states for each Lagrangian frame.
%C A401691 Qutrit stabilizer states and Clifford operations have been experimentally realized in platforms such as trapped ions (Ringbauer et al., 2022) and superconducting circuits (Blok et al., 2021).
%C A401691 This is the qutrit (p = 3) analog of the qubit stabilizer sequence A353196.
%D A401691 L. E. Dickson, Linear Groups: With an Exposition of the Galois Field Theory, Teubner, Leipzig (1901). (For the order of the finite symplectic group).
%H A401691 Jishnu Babu Ranitha, <a href="/A401691/b401691.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..63</a>
%H A401691 M. S. Blok et al., <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevX.11.021010">Quantum Information Scrambling in a Superconducting Qutrit Processor</a>, Phys. Rev. X 11 (2021), 021010; see also <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.03307">arXiv:2003.03307</a> [quant-ph], 2020-2021.
%H A401691 D. Gross, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/1.2393152">Hudson's theorem for finite-dimensional quantum systems</a>, J. Math. Phys. 47 (2006), 122107; see Theorem 20 and Corollary 21.
%H A401691 D. Gross, S. Nezami, and M. Walter, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-021-04118-7">Schur-Weyl Duality for the Clifford Group with Applications: Property Testing, a Robust Hudson Theorem, and de Finetti Representations</a>, Commun. Math. Phys. 385 (2021), 1325-1393; see Eq. (7.4).
%H A401691 M. Ringbauer et al., <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-022-01658-0">A universal qudit quantum processor with trapped ions</a>, Nat. Phys. 18 (2022), 1053-1057; see also <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.06903">arXiv:2109.06903</a> [quant-ph], 2021.
%F A401691 a(n) = 3^n * Product_{i=1..n} (3^i + 1).
%F A401691 a(n) = 3^n * A290000(n+1).
%F A401691 a(n) = 3^n * A323716(n) / 2.
%F A401691 a(n) = 3 * (3^n + 1) * a(n-1) for n >= 1 with a(0) = 1.
%e A401691 a(0) = 1: the empty tensor product (the 0-qutrit state).
%e A401691 a(1) = 12: For a single qutrit, there are 4 linear Lagrangian lines in GF(3)^2 (the 4 mutually unbiased bases: computational basis Z, X, XZ, and XZ^2). Each basis contains 3 orthogonal states, giving 4 * 3 = 12 stabilizer states.
%e A401691 a(2) = 360: On 2 qutrits, there are Product_{i=1..2} (3^i + 1) = (3^1 + 1)*(3^2 + 1) = 4 * 10 = 40 linear Lagrangian planes in GF(3)^4. Each plane has 3^2 = 9 affine cosets, yielding a total of 40 * 9 = 360 stabilizer states.
%t A401691 Table[3^n * Product[3^i + 1, {i, 1, n}], {n, 0, 14}]
%o A401691 (Python)
%o A401691 import math
%o A401691 def a(n):
%o A401691     return (3**n) * math.prod(3**i + 1 for i in range(1, n + 1))
%o A401691 print([a(n) for n in range(15)])
%Y A401691 Cf. A353196 (stabilizer states on n qubits).
%Y A401691 Cf. A290000 (Product_{k=1..n-1} (3^k + 1)), A323716 (Product_{k=0..n} (3^k + 1)).
